Subject: Allow a build to use strerror() instead of strerror_r()

What fails: with -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=600 (needed for other declarations on
AROS, e.g. fdopen() in crypto/rand/randfile.c), crypto/o_str.c calls
strerror_r(), which the ABIv11 C library does not provide: undefined at
link. Dropping the macro instead leaves fdopen() implicitly declared,
truncating its pointer result on 64-bit.

Fix: a build-defined macro, AROS_ST_NO_STRERROR_R, selects OpenSSL's
existing strerror() fallback in openssl_strerror_r(). That branch copies
the message with OPENSSL_strlcpy, so the result is NUL-terminated and
truncated to the caller's buffer; buffers below 2 bytes return failure.

Why this is acceptable here: the build is no-threads and aros-syncthing
is single-threaded. ABIv11 strerror() returns constant strings for
errno values; for values above MAX_ERRNO it formats a DOS error into a
buffer kept in the stdc.library base, which concurrent callers sharing
that base could overwrite. That is a limitation of this configuration,
not a general fix.

Scope: aros-syncthing's OpenSSL build only (macro defined by its Configure
target).

Verification: link of the ABIv11 probe has no undefined symbols; the
build uses -Werror=implicit-function-declaration.

Status: local limitation, not an upstream candidate.
---
--- a/crypto/o_str.c
+++ b/crypto/o_str.c
@@ -400,7 +400,9 @@
     if (err != buf)
         OPENSSL_strlcpy(buf, err, buflen);
     return 1;
-#elif (defined(_POSIX_C_SOURCE) && _POSIX_C_SOURCE >= 200112L) || (defined(_XOPEN_SOURCE) && _XOPEN_SOURCE >= 600)
+#elif !defined(AROS_ST_NO_STRERROR_R) \
+    && ((defined(_POSIX_C_SOURCE) && _POSIX_C_SOURCE >= 200112L) \
+        || (defined(_XOPEN_SOURCE) && _XOPEN_SOURCE >= 600))
     /*
      * We can use "real" strerror_r. The OpenSSL version differs in that it
      * gives 1 on success and 0 on failure for consistency with other OpenSSL
